<TopicPage slug="subjects/petroleum-engineering/foundational/petroleum-geology-and-reservoir-rock">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Petroleum Engineering, and it assumes no geology and no
    engineering — everything is built from scratch. It helps to be comfortable with ratios and
    multiplication, because the subject's arithmetic lives at the pore scale and scales up to
    whole fields. Nothing else is required.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>describe where petroleum comes from — the source rock, the burial "kitchen" and the oil window;</li>
    <li>explain why oil and gas gather in some places and not others: migration, traps and seals;</li>
    <li>define porosity and permeability, say what each measures, and explain why a reservoir needs both;</li>
    <li>read Darcy's law as the profession's list of production levers;</li>
    <li>name the main reservoir fluids and the recovery stages — primary, secondary, enhanced — that get them out.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: every abstract term in this lesson stands for something you could, in principle,
    touch — a rock property, a fluid property, a piece of plumbing. We ask what each thing
    physically is before we ask how it is measured.
  </p>

  <h2>The rock that burns</h2>
  <p>
    Start by correcting the picture most people carry. Petroleum — from the Latin for "rock
    oil" — is not pooled in underground lakes. An oil field is ordinary rock, usually sandstone
    or limestone, whose microscopic pore spaces happen to be full of hydrocarbons, buried a few
    kilometres down at elevated temperature and pressure, with salty water below and often gas
    above. Think of a kitchen sponge soaked in oil and squeezed shut by the weight of everything
    above it: that is a reservoir. Producing it means persuading fluids to creep through pores
    measured in microns, across distances measured in kilometres, against physics that would
    much rather leave them in place. Every piece of petroleum engineering is a negotiation with
    that physics.
  </p>
  <p>
    The stakes are civilisational: the world burns roughly a hundred million barrels of the
    stuff every day, and each barrel began as sunlight captured by plankton. Chemical analysis
    shows how literal that is — crude oil is mostly carbon (83–85 per cent by weight) and
    hydrogen (10–14 per cent), the two elements living things are built from, with small
    amounts of nitrogen, oxygen, sulfur and trace metals. This page follows the system in
    order: how the raw material forms, how it travels, what kind of rock holds it, and what
    kind of fluids fill it.
  </p>

  <h2>From plankton to source rock: making the oil</h2>
  <p>
    Petroleum is fossil sunshine. Ancient seas and lakes teemed with plankton and algae that
    photosynthesised, died, and sank. In most places the remains decayed completely. But in
    basins where the water was starved of oxygen, decay stalled: vast quantities of organic
    goo settled and were buried under successive layers of mud, which slowly became
    <strong>shale</strong> — a fine-grained rock that is often rich in this organic matter and
    dark because of it. That organic-rich shale is the <strong>source rock</strong>, and burial
    is the cooking.
  </p>
  <p>
    As sediments pile kilometres deep, temperature climbs with depth — roughly 25–30 °C per
    kilometre. The solid organic matter, called <strong>kerogen</strong> (a waxy solid you can
    still find in oil shales at the surface), begins to crack thermally into liquid
    hydrocarbons at about 60–120 °C. Geologists call that temperature range the
    <strong>oil window</strong>: below it, the kitchen never lit and the kerogen stays solid;
    above roughly 120–150 °C, the cracking continues past oil into wet gas and then dry gas —
    the <strong>gas window</strong>. Too shallow and nothing happens; too deep and the oil
    overcooks. This is why geologists study <strong>sedimentary basins</strong> — the
    long-lived depressions where kilometres of sediment accumulated — because basins are the
    kitchens: they alone provide the burial history that generates petroleum.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that oil comes from dead dinosaurs. The image is everywhere,
  and it has a grain of truth — petroleum is organic. But the volume arithmetic forbids it: the
  organic matter in oil is overwhelmingly <em>marine plankton and algae</em>, in quantities no
  population of large animals could ever supply, and most oil predates the dinosaurs by
  hundreds of millions of years. A better one-line answer: oil is mostly compressed ancient
  plankton, cooked by burial heat.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> petroleum is plankton buried fast, buried deep, and cooked in a
    narrow thermal window inside source rock. But cooked oil is not a reservoir yet — it has to
    leave the kitchen and find somewhere to stop.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="source, migration, trap, storage, flow" widget="equationStep" config={reservoirCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five panels are the five requirements of a
    <em>petroleum system</em>, and they act like a chain — miss any one and the result is not a
    smaller field but no field at all. A mature source, a migration path, a trap with a seal, a
    porous reservoir to store the fluids, and timing (the trap must exist <em>before</em> the
    hydrocarbons arrive) — the giant fields that anchor national economies are simply places
    where all five coincided, on a scale of billions of barrels.
  </p>

  <h2>Migration and traps: catching a wandering fluid</h2>
  <p>
    Newly generated oil is lighter than the salty water filling the rocks around it (oil is
    roughly 0.8–0.9 g/cm³; formation brine 1.0–1.1), so <strong>buoyancy drives
    migration</strong> — the same force that makes salad oil rise above vinegar. The oil
    squeezes out of the tight source rock and wanders upward and sideways through any permeable
    "carrier bed" it meets, a journey that can cover tens or even hundreds of kilometres,
    steered by underground water flows. Migration is aimless upward wandering until something
    stops it. What stops it is a <strong>trap</strong>: porous rock below, an impermeable
    <strong>seal</strong> above (shale, or best of all rock salt), and a shape that gathers the
    rising fluids.
  </p>
  <p>
    Traps come in two families. <strong>Structural traps</strong> are built by deformation: the
    classic <strong>anticline</strong> is an upward fold whose crest gathers buoyant fluids like
    an upside-down cup (Ghawar in Saudi Arabia, the largest conventional field on Earth, is the
    canonical example), and faults can seal too, smearing clay along the slip plane.
    <strong>Stratigraphic traps</strong> are built by the rocks themselves: a sandstone body
    <em>pinching out</em> into shale, a fossil <strong>reef</strong> of porous carbonate ringed
    by tight mudrock, an unconformity surface capping tilted beds. In every case the fluids
    settle by density into the same arrangement — gas on top, oil beneath it, water at the
    bottom — a three-layer column that every reservoir engineer can picture blind.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that oil sits in underground lakes, rivers or caverns, so a
  well "runs dry" the way a glass empties. Reservoirs are solid rock saturated with fluids;
  there is no pool to pump dry, only pore space to drain — cubic metre by cubic metre, drawn
  from kilometres of rock around the well. This is why production is measured as a gradual
  decline rather than a level dropping, and why a well can flow for decades.</p>

  <h2>Porosity and permeability: the tank and the plumbing</h2>
  <p>
    Two rock properties decide whether a trap is worth drilling, and they are easy to hold
    onto with a sponge. <strong>Porosity</strong> (written φ) is the fraction of the rock's
    volume that is void — how much the sponge <em>holds</em>. It is quoted as a percentage: a
    good sandstone runs 15–30 per cent. Porosity is the tank. Compaction squeezes pores shut
    with depth and cement (quartz or calcite precipitating between grains) destroys them, while
    dissolution can reopen them — which is why two sands deposited identically can store very
    different volumes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Permeability</strong> (written k) is how easily fluid flows through the connected
    pore network — how fast water would move <em>through</em> the sponge. Its unit, the darcy,
    is sized for oilfield rock: a clean, well-connected sandstone runs 0.1–10 darcies (measured
    in millidarcies, mD); a tight gas sand might be 0.01–1 mD; and the organic shales of the
    unconventional revolution measure in nano- to microdarcies — a million to a billion times
    less permeable than a good sand, effectively sealed. The two properties are related but
    <em>not</em> the same: pumice is spectacularly porous and practically impermeable, because
    its bubbles are disconnected; a fractured granite can be nearly zero-porosity and flow like
    a pipe. What couples them is the size and connectedness of pore throats — which is why
    permeability depends steeply on the small fraction of a rock's largest pores.
  </p>
  <p>
    The defining relation is <strong>Darcy's law</strong>: flow rate = permeability × area ×
    pressure drop ÷ (viscosity × length). Read it as the profession's menu of leverage: you
    cannot change the rock, but you <em>can</em> increase the pressure drawdown, attack
    viscosity (heat the oil with steam, dilute with solvent), or manufacture permeability —
    hydraulic fracturing propping open artificial fractures, horizontal wells intersecting
    kilometres of pay. The panel below lets you feel each lever.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="flow response to k, ΔP and viscosity" widget="sliderLab" config={darcyCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag permeability from 10 to 1000 mD at fixed pressure and
    the flow readout multiplies a hundredfold; drag viscosity from 0.5 to 50 cP and it collapses
    by the same factor. That is the entire contrast between a light-oil field and a heavy-oil
    one, in three sliders — and it previews the recovery section below, where steam injection
    and dilution are simply viscosity levers.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real reservoirs add two refinements. When oil and water (or gas) share the pores, each
    obstructs the other — <strong>relative permeability</strong> shows oil flowing at only a
    fraction of its single-phase rate as water saturation rises, which is why water production
    strangles oil wells. And <strong>capillary forces</strong> hold water on grain surfaces and
    control how far buoyant oil can force its way past a seal's pore throats — capillary entry
    pressure is the physics that makes a seal a seal.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a porous rock is automatically a good reservoir — that
  "lots of holes" means "lots of flow". Porosity is storage; permeability is delivery. A rock
  can hold plenty and give none (pumice), or hold little and give it fast (fractured granite).
  The professional question is never "how much pore space?" alone but "how much pore space,
  and how well connected at pore-throat scale?"</p>

  <h2>Reservoir fluids: what is down there</h2>
  <p>
    The fluids are classified by composition and behaviour. <strong>Black oil</strong> — the
    classic medium crude — holds modest dissolved gas; <strong>volatile oils</strong> carry
    much more; <strong>gas condensates</strong> exist as gas underground but drop valuable
    liquids as pressure falls; <strong>dry gas</strong> is nearly pure methane. Within the oil
    itself, four hydrocarbon families mix in proportions that vary field to field: alkanes
    (paraffins), naphthenes (ring-shaped cycloalkanes), aromatics and asphaltics — the heavier
    the mix trends, the thicker and darker the crude.
  </p>
  <p>
    Density is graded on the API scale: light crudes around 35–45 °API flow easily and refine
    richly; heavy oils below 20 °API are syrupy, with viscosities from tens to thousands of
    centipoise, and demand steam or dilution to move at all. Pressure matters as much as
    composition: as a reservoir is produced and pressure falls toward the <strong>bubble
    point</strong>, dissolved gas comes out of solution in the pores — good for the reservoir's
    energy, bad for the oil's flow properties.
  </p>

  <h2>Getting it out: primary, secondary, enhanced</h2>
  <p>
    Production runs in stages. <strong>Primary recovery</strong> rides the reservoir's own
    energy — dissolved gas expanding, a gas cap pushing down, an aquifer pushing up — and
    recovers typically 5–20 per cent of the oil in place before that energy is spent; where
    natural pressure is weak, pumps take over (artificial lift). <strong>Secondary
    recovery</strong> injects water to maintain pressure and sweep oil toward producers —
    waterflooding lifts cumulative recovery toward 30–50 per cent in good reservoirs, and it
    accounts for about half of US production. <strong>Enhanced oil recovery</strong> attacks
    the physics directly: steam thins heavy oil, injected CO<sub>2</sub> mixes with and swells
    the oil, chemical floods lower the interfacial tension that traps blobs of oil in pore
    throats. Even then, roughly half the oil typically stays underground — the industry's
    standing challenge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same physics extends to the unconventional resources now central to supply. Oil sands
    are reservoirs of <em>biodegraded</em> oil: bacteria have eaten the lighter fractions of
    oil that escaped toward the surface, leaving bitumen so thick it must be heated or diluted
    before it flows (Canada's Athabasca deposit; Venezuela's Orinoco extra-heavy oil). Between
    them these two deposits hold an estimated 3.6 trillion barrels — about twice the world's
    conventional oil — but with recovery costs and environmental burdens to match. Reserves
    arithmetic follows from the pore scale upward: oil in place = area × thickness × porosity ×
    (1 − water saturation) ÷ oil volume factor, with the uncertainty quoted as P90/P50/P10
    cases — a geology that arrives as probabilities, not answers.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why is the oil window a window — what happens above and below it?</li>
    <li>Oil is less dense than the brine around it. Why does that guarantee migration, and what ends it?</li>
    <li>Pumice floats on water because it is full of gas bubbles. Would it make a good reservoir rock? Why not?</li>
    <li>A reservoir has produced for years and pressure is fading. Name one secondary and one enhanced-recovery response, and say which physical quantity each attacks.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Below about 60 °C the kerogen has not cracked, so no oil
    forms; above roughly 120–150 °C cracking continues to gas, so the oil overcooks. The window
    is where liquid hydrocarbons survive. (2) Buoyancy pushes the lighter hydrocarbons upward
    through any permeable path until an impermeable seal stops them — the trap. (3) No: pumice
    is porous but its bubbles are disconnected, so permeability is near zero. Storage without
    plumbing. (4) Secondary: waterflood — restores pressure and sweeps oil toward wells
    (attacking the energy and displacement terms). Enhanced: steam injection or CO<sub>2</sub> —
    attacks viscosity (or interfacial tension) to let oil flow through pore throats.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Petroleum is ancient marine plankton buried in oxygen-poor basins and cooked: kerogen cracks to oil at roughly 60–120 °C — the oil window — and to gas above it.</li>
    <li>A petroleum system needs five coincidences: source, migration path, reservoir rock, seal, and timing. Miss one and there is no field.</li>
    <li>Buoyancy drives migration upward until a trap stops it; traps are structural (anticlines, faults) or stratigraphic (pinch-outs, reefs).</li>
    <li>Porosity stores (the tank); permeability flows (the plumbing). A reservoir needs both, and they are not the same property.</li>
    <li>Darcy's law — flow ∝ permeability × pressure drop ÷ viscosity — is the menu of production levers.</li>
    <li>Primary recovery takes 5–20 per cent; waterflooding lifts this toward 30–50 per cent; enhanced methods (steam, CO<sub>2</sub>, chemicals) attack viscosity and tension. About half the oil usually remains.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You now know where the oil is and what holds it. The next lessons build the machinery:
    drilling engineering reaches the rock through kilometres of strata; reservoir engineering
    turns Darcy's law from a sentence into forecasts of production and recovery; production
    engineering manages the fluids once they reach the wellbore. Every one of them begins by
    asking the questions this page fixed: which rock, which fluids, which levers.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One worked calculation shows how pore-scale arithmetic scales to a field. Take a reservoir
    2,000 acres in area, 50 ft thick, 20 per cent porous, with connate water filling 25 per
    cent of the pore space and an oil volume factor of 1.2 (each stock-tank barrel of oil
    occupied 1.2 reservoir barrels underground). Oil initially in place is area × thickness ×
    porosity × (1 − water saturation) ÷ volume factor — in field units, 7758 barrels per
    acre-foot per unit of the product: 7758 × 2,000 × 50 × 0.20 × 0.75 ÷ 1.2 ≈ 97 million
    stock-tank barrels. Every factor is an estimate with its own uncertainty — porosity from
    logs calibrated to cores, saturation from resistivity, volume factor from laboratory
    analysis — which is why the answer is quoted as P90/P50/P10 cases rather than a number.
    The reservoir engineer's career is spent shrinking the error bars on that one
    multiplication.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three deeper threads run from this foundation into modern practice. <strong>Petrophysics
    at a distance</strong>: nobody sees a reservoir; its properties arrive as inference. Well
    logs — gamma ray distinguishing shale from sand, resistivity revealing brine versus
    hydrocarbon, density and neutron porosity — build the model; core samples calibrate it;
    seismic imaging extends it between wells, where most of the uncertainty lives.
    <strong>The unconventional revolution as a porosity–permeability story</strong>: shale
    source rocks are rich but nano-permeable, so for a century they were only ever sources —
    until horizontal drilling plus multi-stage hydraulic fracturing manufactured the missing
    permeability at scale in the 2000s and 2010s, redrawing the global supply map. The lesson
    is the discipline's own: the resource was never missing; the plumbing was. And the
    <strong>portable skill set</strong>: managing fluids in porous media under pressure
    transfers directly to geologic CO<sub>2</sub> storage in saline aquifers, to compressed-air
    and hydrogen storage in depleted fields, and to enhanced geothermal systems — literally
    hydraulic fracturing for heat. Modern petroleum curricula pair Darcy's law with safety,
    economics and energy-transition literacy for exactly this reason: the rock mechanics are
    identical, and the mission is widening. (A historical footnote for the sceptical: an
    abiogenic origin for petroleum — oil from deep inorganic chemistry rather than buried
    organic matter — was proposed in the nineteenth century and is contradicted by the
    geological and geochemical evidence; the biomarkers in crude are unmistakable.)
  </p>

